{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.zero_candidate_descent","record":{"id":"support.zero_candidate_descent","kind":"claim","label":"The zero coefficient is not minimizing","status":"unverified","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","locator":"Long reasoning record #zero-coefficient-example","statement":"For the admissible zero coefficient in pure zero-field SK, q0(t)=2 arcsin(t)/pi and D+P(0)[1]=1/4-1/pi<0. Thus empty Stieltjes support cannot occur at a minimizer. The calculation does not inherit minimizer moment identities.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_admissible"],"citation_ids":["authored_r8_support_zero_candidate_descent"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_deductions_review_20261026"],"boundary":"Ordinary authored deduction checked against retained mathematical inputs; no new Lean declaration, elaborated dependency, cross-model transport, Comparator acceptance or independent proof certification is asserted."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.zero_candidate_descent","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-sanity-check","anchor":"gaussian-sanity-check","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/zero-candidate","anchor":"zero-candidate","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-sanity-check","anchor":"gaussian-sanity-check","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"zero-coefficient-example\"></a>\n\n### 10.3 The zero coefficient\n\nThe admissible coefficient $\\gamma_0=0$ makes the use of minimality in\nthe normalization step explicit. Its diffusion is $X_t=B_t$, and the\n[zero-coefficient heat formula][fs-reg] gives\n\n$$\nu_0(t,x)=2F_{\\mathrm G}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-t}}\\right)-1,\n\\qquad\na_0(t,x)=\\sqrt{\\frac2\\pi}\\frac{e^{-x^2/(2(1-t))}}{\\sqrt{1-t}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbb E e^{-\\lambda B_t^2}=(1+2\\lambda t)^{-1/2}$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb E a_0(t,B_t)=\\sqrt{\\frac2\\pi},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E a_0(t,B_t)^2=\\frac2{\\pi\\sqrt{1-t^2}},\\qquad\nq_0(t)=\\frac2\\pi\\arcsin t.\n$$\n\nThe last identity follows by integrating $q_0'=\\mathbb E a_0^2$\nfrom $q_0(0)=0$. This heat and late-step variation argument is the\nzero-field specialization of [Chen, Handschy and Lerman, Theorem 6\nand its proof, arXiv:1609.04368v4, pp. 13–16](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.04368v4).\nStrict convexity of $\\arcsin$ gives $q_0(t)<t$\nfor $0<t<1$. Thus $G_0(s)=\\int_s^1(q_0(t)-t)\\,dt<0$ for\nevery $s<1$. Inserting an atom at $s$ is admissible, and its\n[first variation][fs-var] is $G_0(s)/2<0$. In particular, the derivative for inserting\nmass at zero is $1/4-1/\\pi<0$. Hence this coefficient does not\nminimize.\n\nHere the Euler scheme is exact: $Y_j=B_{t_j}$ under the Brownian\ncoupling. Nevertheless its normalizers are\n\n$$\nc_j=\\sqrt{\\frac\\pi2}\\,(1-t_{j-1}^2)^{1/4},\n$$\n\nso they approximate this nonconstant function of time, rather than\nconverging uniformly to one. Already $c_1=\\sqrt{\\pi/2}$ for every\nmesh. The directions $g_j$ remain orthonormal by construction and the\nrounding still preserves conditional means; the missing input to the\nvalue limit is the minimizing identity $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$.\n\nThe terminal spin also survives:\n$U_1=\\operatorname{sgn}(B_1)$ almost surely. But its correlation is\n\n$$\n\\mathbb E[U_1B_1]=\\sqrt{\\frac2\\pi}=\\mathcal P(\\gamma_0),\n$$\n\nthe value of this nonminimizing candidate. The negative first variation\nproves that this value is strictly above $P_*$. Admissibility, exact\nEuler sampling, and a spin-valued terminal martingale therefore do not\nsupply the minimizing normalization or identify the ground-state value.\n","span":{"start_byte":180696,"end_byte":182843,"start_line":3861,"end_line":3919},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-sanity-check","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-sanity-check"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n### 5.4 The remaining initial and terminal gaps\n\n**Initial gap with zero outside the support.** Suppose $[0,s)$ is\nmissing and $s<1$ is the first support point. Then $\\gamma=0$ there,\nso the positive-coefficient bound supplies no strictness. Instead the\nzero-coefficient heat identity gives\n\n$$\nq''(t)=\\mathbb E[\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2]>0\\qquad(0<t<s).\n$$\n\nHere is why the inequality is strict. On this interval $X_t=B_t$,\nwhose density is positive everywhere at positive times. If the expected\nsquare were zero, continuity would force $\\Phi_{xxx}(t,x)=0$ for\nevery $x$: a nonzero value would give a small interval on which the\nsquare is positive, and that interval has positive probability.\nThen $\\Phi(t,\\cdot)$ would be a quadratic polynomial, and its bounded\nslope would make it affine, a linear function plus a constant.\nBut choosing zero control gives\n$\\Phi_\\gamma(t,x)\\ge\\mathbb E|x+B_1-B_t|\\ge|x|$: the average\nabsolute value is at least the absolute value of the average. This\nrules out an affine value: domination of $|x|$ in the positive tail\nrequires slope at least one, while the negative tail requires slope\nat most minus one. The heat identity and the needed regularity are\nin [OpenAI (2026a), Lemmas 2.2–2.3][support-regularity].\n\nThus $q'$ is strictly increasing, and right contact gives\n$q'(t)<q'(s)\\le1$ for $0<t<s$. Symmetry supplies $q(0)=0$,\nwhile contact gives $q(s)=s$. Integrating again gives too little area.\nStrictness was needed only at positive times; symmetry can make the\nthird spatial derivative vanish at $(0,0)$.\n\nAlthough the forward path is Brownian here, the backward value receives\ndata $\\Phi_\\gamma(s,\\cdot)$ from the later coefficient. The formula\nfor the globally zero candidate cannot be reused on this initial\ninterval. The case where zero itself is supported was already included\nin Section 5.3, using its atom and the one-sided slope bound.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\noindent\\begin{minipage}{\\linewidth}\n```\n\n**Empty support.** This would mean $\\gamma=0$ everywhere. The zero\ncandidate fails the first-variation test:\n\n$$\nD\\mathcal P(0)[1]=\\frac14-\\frac1\\pi<0.\n$$\n\nThe direction adds an atom at zero and is admissible. Section 7.1\ncomputes the derivative from the explicit Gaussian response; no\nminimiser identity is used. A minimiser therefore has nonempty support.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\end{minipage}\n```\n\n**Terminal gap.** Now suppose a maximal missing interval is $(r,1)$.\nIts left endpoint $r<1$ is supported, and its constant height is\npositive, by the same mass argument as in Section 5.3. We have\n$q(r)=r$ and $q'(r)\\le1$, and $q'$ is strictly convex on $(r,1)$.\nThere is no contact condition at one. We use the bound $q\\le1$\ninstead. We will show that $q$ stays below the diagonal throughout\nthe gap, which makes the tail cost $G(r)$ negative. If it reached\nthe diagonal, the area comparison would force its slope above one;\nconvexity would preserve enough of that excess to violate $q\\le1$.\n\nPut $h(t)=q'(t)-1$, so $h(r)\\le0$. If $q(t)\\ge t$ at some\n$r<t<1$, strict integration below the chord on the finite interval\n$[r,t]$ would give\n\n$$\n0\\le q(t)-t=\\int_r^t h(v)\\,dv\n<\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(h(r)+h(t)\\bigr)\n\\le\\frac{t-r}{2}h(t).\n$$\n\nHence $h(t)>0$. To control a later slope, use the same chord rule\non the larger interval $[r,s]$, where $t<s<1$. At its interior\npoint $t$ this gives\n\n$$\nh(t)\\le\\frac{s-t}{s-r}h(r)+\\frac{t-r}{s-r}h(s).\n$$\n\nSolving for the right endpoint gives\n\n$$\nh(s)\\ge h(t)+\\frac{s-t}{t-r}\\bigl(h(t)-h(r)\\bigr)\\ge h(t)>0.\n$$\n\nConsequently $q(s)\\ge t+(s-t)(1+h(t))$. Choose any time\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{1-t}{1+h(t)}<s<1.\n$$\n\nSuch a choice exists because $h(t)>0$, and the lower bound now gives\n$q(s)>1$, contradicting $q(s)\\le1$ strictly before time one.\nThus $q(t)<t$ throughout $(r,1)$, giving\n$G(r)=\\int_r^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt<0$, contrary to the addition test.\nOnly finite-interval convexity and the bound on $q$ were used; no\nvalue for $q'(1)$ was assumed.\n\nThe source's different terminal argument proves the stronger estimate\n$1-q(t)\\ge C\\sqrt{1-t}$ near one for any finite constant terminal\ncoefficient, without the left contact data used here. Its proof is\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3][support-variation] and is retained in the\n*Long Reasoning Record*,\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"terminal-gap\">[§5, terminal estimate](../long/reasoning-record.md#terminal-gap)</span>.\n[Auffinger–Chen–Zeng, Remark 7][acz] had already recorded the absence\nof terminal plateaus.\n\nThese cases exhaust the complement of the support. Contact now gives\n$q(t)=t$ for every interior time, and symmetry includes zero.\nDifferentiating gives $\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^2]=1$, completing the theorem.\n","span":{"start_byte":33990,"end_byte":38668,"start_line":795,"end_line":911},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/expository?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=endpoints","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=endpoints"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/zero-candidate","anchor":"zero-candidate","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n### 7.1 Why the zero candidate does not minimise\n\nReturn to the Gaussian value and slope from Section 3. With $\\gamma=0$,\nthe state is $B_t$. Differentiating the slope once more gives\n\n$$\nX_t=B_t,\\qquad\nu_0(t,x)=2F_{\\mathrm G}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-t}}\\right)-1,\n\\qquad\na_0(t,x)=\\sqrt{\\frac2{\\pi(1-t)}}e^{-x^2/(2(1-t))}.\n$$\n\nA Gaussian integral gives\n$\\mathbb E e^{-B_t^2/(1-t)}=\\sqrt{(1-t)/(1+t)}$. Substituting\ninto $q_0'=\\mathbb E[a_0(t,B_t)^2]$ and integrating from zero yields\n\n$$\nq_0'(t)=\\frac2{\\pi\\sqrt{1-t^2}},\\qquad\nq_0(t)=\\frac2\\pi\\arcsin t.\n$$\n\nThis recovers the half-time value $1/3$. It also shows that\n$q_0(t)\\to1$ while $q_0'(t)\\to\\infty$: convergence of a function\nat one does not bound its derivative there.\nThe constant direction $\\eta=1$ is admissible and adds an atom at zero.\nIts cost is\n\n$$\nD\\mathcal P(0)[1]\n=\\frac12\\int_0^1\\left(\\frac2\\pi\\arcsin t-t\\right)dt\n=\\frac14-\\frac1\\pi<0.\n$$\n\nSo a small positive constant coefficient improves the zero candidate.\nThis calculation uses the heat and variation formulas, not a minimiser\nhypothesis; see also [Chen–Handschy–Lerman, proof of Theorem 6][chl-optimality].\n","span":{"start_byte":41626,"end_byte":42764,"start_line":979,"end_line":1015},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/expository?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=zero-candidate","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=zero-candidate"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/gr